                DS name UD_GLB_BFC_STAT; the same register exists in the up port (vendor U_GLB_BFC_STAT) and in the down port at +0x2000 (vendor D_GLB_BFC_STAT).

                Data sheet (Hi3520D/Hi3515A/Hi3515C data sheet issue 07 (2015-02-09)): section 5.1.6.3, Table 5-7, page 5-53, register description at text dump line 27164 (build/sdk-survey/datasheet.txt).
            ";

            field {
                name = "timerover_cnt";
                desc = "Register for the count of multi-packet interrupt aging time counter overflow events (the count reaches the configured value). Note: If the value of timerover_cnt is too large in a unit of time, it indicates that UD_GLB_IRQN_SET[int_frm_cnt] is set improperly. Multi-packet interrupt is triggered by the aging time. Therefore, the configured value must be reduced.";
                sw = r;
                hw = w;
                reset = 0x0000;
            } timerover_cnt[31:16];

            field {
                name = "flowctrl_cnt";
                desc = "Register for the count of the forward buffer of the uplink or downlink port entering the traffic control status. Note: If the value of flowctrl_cnt is too large in a unit of time, it indicates that UD_GLB_FC_LEVEL[blimit_up] (sic, the field is qlimit_up) or UD_GLB_FC_LEVEL[blimit_down] (qlimit_down) is set to a too small value, or the external network condition is worsened. In this case, the configured value may be reduced.";
                sw = r;
                hw = w;
                reset = 0x0000;
            } flowctrl_cnt[15:0];

        } GLB_BFC_STAT @ 0x35C;

        reg {
            name = "TX queue header address register";
            desc = "
                UD_GLB_EQ_ADDR is a TX queue header address register. The register does not support soft reset. If the header address of the TX frame is not word aligned, the logic reads data according to the word aligned address. In this case, the previously read data is invalid and discarded. For example, if the configured header address of the TX frame is 0xF000_8102 (non-word-aligned address), the logic directly discards the byte data read from the 0xF000_8100 and 0xF000_8101 addresses. Then, the logic considers the data read from the 0xF000_8102 address as the first byte (valid data) of the TX frame and considers the data read from the 0xF000_8103 address as the second byte (valid data) of the TX frame. All subsequent data is valid (until the data of the specified frame length is read). If the configured header address of the TX frame is other non-word-aligned address, the logic reads data in a similar way.

                DS name UD_GLB_EQ_ADDR; the same register exists in the up port (vendor U_GLB_EQ_ADDR) and in the down port at +0x2000 (vendor D_GLB_EQ_ADDR).

                Data sheet (Hi3520D/Hi3515A/Hi3515C data sheet issue 07 (2015-02-09)): section 5.1.6.3, Table 5-7, page 5-53, register description at text dump line 27205 (build/sdk-survey/datasheet.txt).
            ";

            field {
                name = "add_fd_addr_out";
                desc = "Header address of the TX frame added by the CPU to the TX queue.";
                reset = 0x00000000;
            } add_fd_addr_out[31:0];

        } GLB_EQ_ADDR @ 0x360;

        reg {
            name = "TX queue frame length configuration register";
            desc = "
                UD_GLB_EQFRM_LEN is a TX queue frame length configuration register. The register does not support soft reset.

                DS name UD_GLB_EQFRM_LEN; the same register exists in the up port (vendor U_GLB_EQFRM_LEN) and in the down port at +0x2000 (vendor D_GLB_EQFRM_LEN).

                Data sheet (Hi3520D/Hi3515A/Hi3515C data sheet issue 07 (2015-02-09)): section 5.1.6.3, Table 5-7, page 5-54, register description at text dump line 27237 (build/sdk-survey/datasheet.txt).
            ";

            field {
                name = "add_fd_len_out";
                desc = "Length of the TX frame added by the CPU to the TX queue. Configure this register to trigger hardware so that software can write the header address and length of the TX frame to the TX queue for transmission. When transmitting a frame, software must write the header address of the frame before the length of the frame. Note: The frames whose add_fd_len_out is less than 20 bytes or greater than 1600 bytes are discarded. In other words, the allowed range is from 20 bytes to 1600 bytes.";
                reset = 0x000;
            } add_fd_len_out[10:0];

        } GLB_EQFRM_LEN @ 0x364;

        reg {
